eric  statistics problem signal plus noise or  estimate parameters of a machine  learning model  um and uh basic resources  data and computation so like i said  mathematical statistics  is concerned with how much data do we  need but it says nothing about  computational complexity  at the same time the field of  computational complexity has been  concerned with  how much computation do i need to solve  some problem  and says very little about the sorts of  practical  uh real-life machine learning and  statistics problems that we're faced  with  because those problems have data that is  benign in some sense and most of  computational complexity concerns  worst-case  uh worst case problems of course with  that said i mean there is  of course the field of average case  complexity and also cryptography studies  average case problems  but most of what's been done there  doesn't apply to the sort of statistics  problems that statisticians are faced  with  now what's intriguing is that these two  resources actually  interplay one with another and so these  problems can't be studied only from one  perspective  one actually needs to combine the two  perspectives in some way and so at the  high level that's what the aim of this  program is  is to understand how do these two  resources interplay  how much of each of them do i need  for each sort of problem that i'm  interested in  so i'll give a sort of a case study here  in this next slide  this case study is an example problem  for which  there is a pretty satisfactory  understanding and this understanding is  captured  by what's can be called a feasibility  diagram  and so on the vertical axis we have a  signal strength parameter  as we go up the signal gets weaker on  the horizontal axis  there's a parameter capturing the  sparsity of the signal you can think of  it generically  just something that's controlling the  combinatorial structure of the signal  that one wishes to recover  and over roughly a decade of intense  study by many many researchers  the following feasibility diagram has  emerged  and what it does at the highest level is  partitions the set of possible  the set of parameters of the problem  into three regions  one where the problem is just impossible  meaning it's impossible even with  infinite computational power there just  isn't enough signal  information is too weak there's an easy  regime  where an efficient algorithm often  linear time or  low low order polynomial can solve the  problem  and then there's a hard regime where  it's believed to be hard and there's  various forms of evidence indicating  that it's computationally hard  but it is feasible in principle if one  is willing to invest massive amounts of  computational  power and so this problem is pretty well  understood because there's this  kind of demarcation of every possible  parameter  into one of the three regimes and so  this is a success  of the of the field but the concern is  that this took a decade of work  by a lot of people and dozens of papers  on the topic  there are one minute many dozens of  problems  uh exhibit this sort of behavior  and so this motivates the question how  the world do we understand this  landscape  of problems and how do we understand  their computational feasibility  as a function of how much data we have  or vice versa how much  data do we need for computationally  efficient algorithms  the broad objectives of the field in a  nutshell are one  what is the amount of data that needs is  needed by computationally efficient  algorithms  two if we have notions of optimality  that are supported by item one then what  are those  optimal algorithms that achieve this  minimum  more broadly can we understand  relationships between problems as  opposed to setting problems in isolation  which is what's traditionally done  in uh in statistics and can we elicit  relationships between  classes of algorithms instead of just  applying every possible algorithm to  every  statistical 